swbrd-ext-echo
==============

An extension is any process listening on a Unix socket that the daemon's
directory names.  The daemon dials it once per datachannel, sends a control
frame naming the peer, and then relays; the connection's lifetime is the
channel's.

This one answers the `echo.v1` protocol, which /etc/swbrd/directory.d/10-echo.toml
maps here.  Point a browser at tests/channels.html to exercise it.

Backpressure needs no cooperation from an extension: read slowly and the daemon
stops reading the peer.  Stop reading altogether and the daemon holds nothing,
then tears the circuit down once `stall_ms` from the directory entry has passed.
